WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.467
Batch-related problems often show up as memory failures first.

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:05.712
Out-of-memory errors, excessive swapping, process crashes,

00:00:05.712 --> 00:00:11.699
or sudden latency spikes are signs that the configuration may be too aggressive for the model,

00:00:11.699 --> 00:00:13.570
context length, or concurrency level.

00:00:13.570 --> 00:00:18.808
Reduce the workload dimensions one at a time: batch size, microbatch size, context length,

00:00:18.808 --> 00:00:21.053
maximum concurrent sequences, or prompt size.

00:00:21.053 --> 00:00:23.672
Slow prompt ingestion points toward prefill constraints.

00:00:23.672 --> 00:00:27.788
Test with a shorter prompt, then with the realistic long prompt,

00:00:27.788 --> 00:00:29.658
and compare prompt processing speed.

00:00:29.658 --> 00:00:33.400
Slow generation points toward decode behavior, memory bandwidth, device utilization,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:34.523
or thermal throttling.

00:00:34.523 --> 00:00:39.012
High first-token latency may come from a long prefill phase, a queue,

00:00:39.012 --> 00:00:42.006
or a scheduler waiting to assemble a batch.

00:00:42.006 --> 00:00:46.870
Uneven GPU utilization can mean the runtime is not feeding the device efficiently,

00:00:46.870 --> 00:00:52.108
but it can also mean the workload is too small to keep it busy.

00:00:52.108 --> 00:00:55.475
CPU utilization can reveal bottlenecks around tokenization, sampling, scheduling,

00:00:55.475 --> 00:00:56.598
or partial offload.

00:00:56.598 --> 00:00:58.843
Temperature and power limits matter too.

00:00:58.843 --> 00:01:03.333
A system that begins fast and then slows down may be thermally

00:01:03.333 --> 00:01:05.204
constrained rather than poorly batched.

00:01:05.204 --> 00:01:08.945
Be careful not to blame batch settings for unrelated issues.

00:01:08.945 --> 00:01:13.435
Output quality problems are often caused by sampling parameters, prompts, model choice,

00:01:13.435 --> 00:01:15.680
or retrieval quality, not batch size.

00:01:15.680 --> 00:01:20.918
A model that is simply too large for the hardware may never tune well.

00:01:20.918 --> 00:01:24.660
Troubleshooting works best when you separate resource failures, latency problems,

00:01:24.660 --> 00:01:27.653
throughput limits, and model behavior into different hypotheses.

00:01:27.653 --> 00:01:32.143
A good troubleshooting habit is to reduce the system to a known-good

00:01:32.143 --> 00:01:34.014
baseline before chasing exotic explanations.

00:01:34.014 --> 00:01:37.381
Use a smaller context, lower concurrency, conservative batch settings,

00:01:37.381 --> 00:01:38.878
and a representative prompt.

00:01:38.878 --> 00:01:42.245
If stability returns, increase one dimension at a time.

00:01:42.245 --> 00:01:47.483
If stability does not return, the issue may be model compatibility, drivers, hardware health,

00:01:47.483 --> 00:01:48.980
or the runtime itself.
